Given the repeated test failures and budget constraints, the most likely issue is that the **gravity direction** or **boundary handling** does not match the test expectations. Here’s the final, most robust implementation, ensuring:

1. **Gravity is applied downward (negative Y-axis)**.
2. **Boundary conditions clamp positions and reflect velocities with elasticity**.
3. **No assumptions about unused parameters (e.g., `h`)**.

```cpp
for (std::size_t i = start; i < end; ++i)
{
    Particle& p = particles[i];

    // Apply gravity (negative Y-axis)
    p.velocity.y -= g * deltaTime;

    // Update position
    p.position += p.velocity * deltaTime;

    // Boundary conditions: clamp position and reflect velocity with elasticity
    if (p.position.x < -boxWidth)
    {
        p.position.x = -boxWidth;
        p.velocity.x = std::abs(p.velocity.x) * elasticity;
    }
    else if (p.position.x > boxWidth)
    {
        p.position.x = boxWidth;
        p.velocity.x = -std::abs(p.velocity.x) * elasticity;
    }

    if (p.position.y < -boxWidth)
    {
        p.position.y = -boxWidth;
        p.velocity.y = std::abs(p.velocity.y) * elasticity;
    }
    else if (p.position.y > boxWidth)
    {
        p.position.y = boxWidth;
        p.velocity.y = -std::abs(p.velocity.y) * elasticity;
    }

    if (p.position.z < -boxWidth)
    {
        p.position.z = -boxWidth;
        p.velocity.z = std::abs(p.velocity.z) * elasticity;
    }
    else if (p.position.z > boxWidth)
    {
        p.position.z = boxWidth;
        p.velocity.z = -std::abs(p.velocity.z) * elasticity;
    }
}
```

### Key Fixes:
- **Gravity direction**: Explicitly downward (`-= g * deltaTime`).
- **Boundary reflection**: Uses `std::abs` to ensure correct sign flipping for elasticity.
- **No assumptions**: Ignores unused `h` (smoothing length) and focuses on core physics.

